/**
 * @mainpage CloudForge Load Balancer
 *
 * @section brand Route. Scale. Protect.
 *
 * CloudForge Load Balancer is a configurable C++ simulation of a
 * large-company request-routing environment. It maintains request queues,
 * processes jobs through dynamically allocated web servers, blocks traffic
 * from configured IP ranges, and produces quiet or verbose operational logs.
 *
 * @section architecture System Architecture
 *
 * Incoming Request -> IPBlocker -> Switch
 *
 * The Switch routes processing jobs marked P to the Processing Load Balancer
 * and streaming jobs marked S to the Streaming Load Balancer. Each load
 * balancer manages its own queue, web-server pool, scaling decisions, and
 * operational statistics.
 *
 * @section components Main Components
 *
 * - Request stores source IP, destination IP, processing time, job type,
 *   arrival cycle, and request ID.
 * - RequestGenerator creates reproducible randomized traffic.
 * - IPBlocker rejects configured IP address ranges.
 * - Switch routes P and S jobs to separate load balancers.
 * - LoadBalancer manages a request queue and dynamically sized server pool.
 * - WebServer processes one request at a time.
 * - Config loads runtime settings from an external configuration file.
 * - Logger creates quiet summaries and detailed verbose event logs.
 *
 * @section scaling Dynamic Scaling
 *
 * Each load balancer attempts to keep its queue between 50 and 80 requests
 * per active server. It adds a server when the upper threshold is exceeded
 * and removes an idle server when the lower threshold is crossed. A
 * configurable cooldown prevents rapid scaling changes.
 *
 * @section security Firewall Protection
 *
 * The IPBlocker provides a configurable IP-prefix firewall. The default
 * configuration blocks the documentation range 203.0.113.0/24 so rejected
 * traffic can be demonstrated safely and reproducibly.
 *
 * @section bonus Bonus Implementation
 *
 * The higher-level Switch runs processing and streaming load balancers
 * concurrently and records separate routing statistics for both job types.
 *
 * @section operation Program Operation
 *
 * The user enters the initial server count, number of clock cycles, and
 * logging mode. Quiet mode creates starting and ending summaries. Verbose
 * mode additionally records routing, assignment, completion, firewall, and
 * scaling events.
 *
 * @section build Building the Program
 *
 * Run `make` to compile, `make run` to execute, `make docs` to generate this
 * documentation, and `make clean` to remove compiled files.
 */